# Tandem Solar Cells Made from Amorphous Silicon and Polymer Bulk Heterojunction Sub-Cells

## abstract
A tandem solar cell based on a combination of an amorphous silicon (a-Si) and polymer solar cell (PSC) is demonstrated. As these tandem devices can be readily fabricated by low-cost methods, they require only a minor increase in the total manufacturing cost. Therefore, a combination of a-Si and PSC provides a compelling solution to reduce the cost of electricity produced by photovoltaics.

Judging by the increased industrial production of thin-film photovoltaic (PV) devices, it is evident that these represent the next step in the evolution of photovoltaics. 1–4  Although a number of thin-film PV technologies have been developed, such as cadmium telluride (CdTe), 5–8  copper–indium–gallium–selenide (CIGS), 9–13  polymer bulk heterojunction (BHJ), 14–18  dye-sensitized solar cells (DSSCs), 19–22  etc.; amorphous silicon (a-Si) was the first to be produced on a large scale and offers a number of advantages in terms of its low cost, high mobility, and high stability in sunlight. 23–25  However, for a-Si solar panels to compete with crystalline silicon and other thinfilm technologies, a cost-effective method for increasing the power conversion efficiency (PCE) is needed. The combination of polymer BHJ solar cells with a-Si solar cells offers a novel, rapid, and scalable method for cost-effectively increasing the PCE of a-Si solar cells.
Arguably, the biggest limitation of a-Si cells is that their band gap of ${\approx}1.75~\mathrm{eV}$ renders a significant portion of the solar spectrum unusable by a single junction solar panel. 26,27  Thus, in order to effectively tap this unused part of the spectrum, additional photovoltaic stacks of other inorganic materials such as microcrystalline silicon (mc-Si) are often placed in tandem. 28–30 As mc-Si has a band gap of $1.1\ \mathrm{eV},$ it is able to absorb photons in the $1.1\mathrm{-}1.75~\mathrm{eV}$ energy range, thereby increasing the overall PCE of the cell. 28–30  However, the more expensive microcrystalline layers and additional conducting oxide layers required greatly increase the manufacturing cost; and as a result, the levelized cost of electricity in terms of dollar per kilowatt-hour is only marginally reduced. It is also worth noting here that the thinner active layer produced by placing additional PV stacks on top of a single junction a-Si panel reduces the Staebler–Wronski effect that is responsible for a degradation in performance with prolonged exposure to UV light. 24,29,31  This interesting phenomenon has motivated the design of thin $(\approx200\ \mathrm{nm})$ layers of single a-Si p-i-n junctions stacked together into a tandem device (a-Si/a-Si) instead of having a single p-i-n junction of the same total thickness. Light soaking also presents a vexing problem, as the resulting drop in efficiency is directly reflected in a higher levelized cost of electricity. 32
While it is clear that higher efficiency tandem-junction a-Si solar panels are an attractive route to achieving lower costs, there is still a need for cost-effective methods for depositing additional PV layers on single-junction a-Si solar panels. 33–36 A unique solution to this problem is the use of organic semiconducting materials based on conjugated polymers, dye molecules, etc., with polymer solar cells (PSCs) in particular offering two distinct advantages: they can be cheaply and easily produced by coating or printing from a liquid state, and their molecular structure of conjugated polymers can be altered through synthetic chemistry to fine-tune the electronic structure to absorb any particular part of the solar spectrum. 15,37  The combination of these two advantages makes tandem a-Si/polymer solar cells a compelling option for bringing down the cost of electricity produced by photovoltaics. This study represents a proof of concept for a tandem device made up of a-Si and PSC sub-cells. The results presented here were obtained by fabricating a PSC made from semiconducting polymers and (methano) fullerenes directly on top of an a-Si solar cell, which represents only a minimal increase in the total manufacturing cost.
Figure 1a shows the device architecture of an a-Si/PSC tandem cell, which in this study was produced through plasmaenhanced chemical vapor deposition (PECVD) on glass/indium tin oxide (ITO) substrates. The various layers that make up the a-Si solar cell; namely p-doped hydrogenated a-Si, an intermediate layer of hydrogenated a-Si, n-doped hydrogenated a-Si, and a top ITO layer; were sequentially deposited and suitably etched to expose the bottom ITO layer. Single-junction polymer BHJ solar cells were then fabricated on an ITO-coated glass substrate by sequential solution state deposition of the following layers: a PEDOT:PSS (Baytron PH) conducting interlayer, a BHJ layer consisting of a low band-gap copolymer PSBTBT 38,39 - $c o$ -PCDTBT 16  and [6,6]-phenyl C71 butyric acid methyl ester $(\mathrm{PC}_{70}\mathrm{BM})$ , and a titanium sub-oxide $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{x}\right)$ optical spacer. 40  After assembly of the tandem cell, an aluminum electrode was deposited by thermal evaporation.
The structure of the tandem cell consists of an $\mathrm{{ITO/p\mathrm{{\cdot}S i/i\mathrm{{\cdot}}}}}$ Si/n-Si/ITO a-Si front-cell and an ITO/PEDOT:PSS/PSBTBTco-PCDTBT: $\mathrm{PC}_{70}\mathrm{BM}$ BHJ/ $\mathrm{\DeltaTiO}_{x}/\mathrm{Al}$ PSC back-cell, which means that the a-Si cells show high transmittance in the range of wavelengths greater than $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ , is offset by the high absorbance of the PSBTBT-co-PCDTBT: $\mathrm{PC}_{70}\mathrm{BM}$ BHJ film between 900 and $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ , as shown in Figure 1b. When irradiated by light, negative-charge carriers (electrons) generated in the a-Si front-cell should move from the $\mathrm{n}{\cdot}\mathrm{Si}$ to p-Si layer due to the Fermi-level difference between them, while electrons in the PSC back-cell are accelerated to the Al electrode by the built-in field (BIF) arising from the Fermi-level difference between the ITO anode and Al cathode. This is evidenced by the energy-level diagram of the tandem cell components shown in Figure 1c, and means that the device polarity of the a-Si front-cell can be easily switched by changing the relative position of the p-Si and n-Si layer. Similarly, the polarity of the PSC back-cell can also be switched by inverting its structure. 41
For this study, two prototype tandem dual-junction solar cells were produced: a series device based on a structure of glass/ $\mathrm{ITO/p\mathrm{-}S i/i\mathrm{-}S i/n\mathrm{-}S i/\mathrm{ITO/PEDOT/BH//TiO_{x}/A l}}$ (Figure 2a), and a parallel device based on a glass/ITO/n-Si/i-Si/p-Si/ITO/PEDOT/BHJ/ $\mathrm{TiO}_{x}/\mathrm{Al}$ structure (Figure 2b). Note that the device structure of both devices is effectively the same, the notable exception being the position of the p-Si and n-Si layers: the series tandem cell has a p-i-n junction structure, whereas the parallel cell is an n-i-p junction device. In the series tandem device, electrons generated in the a-Si front-cell move to the shared ITO layer, where they then recombine with holes generated in the PSC back-cell to increase the overall open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of the device; that is, the total $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ is the sum of the two sub-cells. 42–44  This also means that the current is determined by the lesser value of the two sub-cells, which in this instance is the BHJ back-cell. In contrast, the reversal of the p-Si and n-Si layers in the parallel device means that holes move from the a-Si front-cell to the common ITO layer and add to the holes generated in the PSC back-cell. As a result, the total current in this instance is determined by the sum of that generated in each cell, thereby increasing the short-circuit current (Jsc). 45,46
Figure 3a shows the current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ characteristics of the a-Si front-cell, the PSC back-cell, and their combined effect in a series tandem cell. These were separately measured by connecting the common ITO electrode to the outer electrodes, bottom ITO layer, and Al electrode, respectively. We see from this that the p-i-n structure of the a-Si front-cell yields a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}=7.2\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=0.58~\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{i}}$ , $\mathrm{FF}=0.65$ , and $\mathrm{PCE}=2.71\%$ ; the values that are comparable to those of a single a-Si cell with no PSC deposition. In contrast, the PSBTBT- $c o$ -PCDTBT backcell exhibits a relatively poor performance in terms of having a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}=3.3~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=0.58\:\mathrm{V}$ $\mathrm{FF}=0.68$ , and $\mathrm{{PCE}=}$ $1.3\%$ . Given that incident photons are first absorbed in the a-Si front-cell, this low $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of the PSC back-cell can be ascribed to a decrease in photon absorption. Consequently, improving the PCE of a series tandem cell requires increasing the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of the PSC back-cell, which should be possible by using polymers with a lower band-gap and minimizing the overlap in the absorption spectrum of the two layers. However, despite the low PCE of the PSC, the tandem cell exhibits a greatly enhanced performance of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}=3.9\mathrm{mAcm^{-2}}$ $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=1.15\:\mathrm{V},$ and $\mathrm{FF}=0.7$ . Furthermore, its PCE of $3.14\%$ is $20\%$ higher than that of single a-Si cell. It is also particularly notable that the open-circuit voltages of the p-i-n a-Si and polymer BHJ sub-cells add perfectly in the series tandem device. This lossless addition is attributed to the liquid-state processing of the conducting interlayer between the BHJ and ITO layer of the a-Si sub-cell.
In order to investigate the relative contribution of each subcell to the overall current generation, the incident photon to current efficiency (IPCE) was measured and the photoresponses of the a-Si sub-cell, PSC sub-cell, and series tandem cell are given as open-circle, closed-circle, and black-line, respectively, in Figure 3b. As expected from the transmittance and absorption spectra in Figure 1b, the a-Si sub-cell clearly starts generating photocurrent from a wavelength of $700~\mathrm{nm}$ , and gives a maximum IPCE of $\approx50\%$ at $500~\mathrm{nm}$ . In contrast, the PSC exhibits two dominant peaks at 750 and $820~\mathrm{nm}$ , with IPCEs of $\approx35\%$ and ${\approx}25\%$ , respectively. When these two sub-cells are combined, the resulting series tandem cell is capable of producing photocurrent from wavelengths ranging from 900 to $350\mathrm{nm}$ .
If one considers the low $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ generated in the tandem device by the much lower $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of the PSC back-cell, then it is clear that a parallel device is conceptually a far more straightforward approach to enhance the efficiency of tandem solar cells. For instance, since the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of the two sub-cells is simply added, there is no need for the current matching conditions required with series-tandem devices. $[46,47]$ Moreover, given that the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of PSBTBT-co-PCDTBT PSC is equal to that of a-Si, there should be no loss of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ when the sub-cells are combined in parallel. This is confirmed by Figure 4a, which shows the $J{-}V$ characteristics of an n-i-p-structured a-Si front-cell (open-circle), PSC back-cell (closed-circle), and parallel tandem cell (open-lozenge); while the black line represents the current as calculated by a simple summation of the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of the two sub-cells. It can be seen from this that the a-Si front-cell yields a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}=6.67\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=0.56$ V, $\mathrm{FF}=0.55$ , and $\mathrm{PCE}=2.05\%$ ; the values that are almost equal to those of a single a-Si cell with no PSC deposition. Meanwhile, the PSBTBT-co-PCDTBT back-cell yields a
$J_{\mathrm{sc}}=4.4~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=0.56$ V, $\mathrm{FF}=0.6$ , and $\mathrm{PCE}=1.45\%$ , and thus, the small difference in the performance of the PSC sub-cell between series and parallel configurations can be attributed to the change in structure of the a-Si front-cell. More importantly, when compared with a single a-Si cell, this parallel tandem cell exhibits enhanced performance with a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ $=10.8\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=0.56\mathrm{~V_{\mathrm{z}}}$ , $\mathrm{FF}=0.53$ , and a $60\%$ greater PCE of $3.2\%$ . This enhanced performance is the direct result of the increase in the short-circuit current and absence of any discernible $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ loss, which is evident in the similarity between the calculated (black line) and measured (open-lozenge) current densities in Figure 4a. It is also further confirmed by the IPCE measurement results of the a-Si cell, PSC cell, and parallel tandem cell shown in Figure 4b as open-circle, closedcircle, and open-lozenge, respectively. The black line represents the IPCE value calculated from the linear sum of the individual sub-cell IPCE values, and clearly shows that the IPCE spectra of the parallel tandem cell are also a simple superposition of the IPCE spectra of the two sub-cells.
In conclusion, we have successfully demonstrated the fabrication of a tandem solar-cell device based on forming a polymer solar cell on top of an a-Si solar cell. When fabricated in series, this tandem cell exhibits a $20\%$ greater efficiency than a single a-Si cell, with its total open-circuit voltage being the perfect sum of the two individual sub-cells. A parallel tandem cell, on the other hand, exhibits a $60\%$ higher efficiency and near-perfect addition of the current densities of the two sub-cells. Either of these tandem cells can be readily made with only one additional low-cost processing step, and therefore require only a minimal increase in total manufacturing cost. Moreover, the absorption spectrum of the polymer back-cell can be easily changed by altering its conjugated structure. This tandem cell design is therefore expected to provide a suitable means of reducing the cost of energy produced by photovoltaics.
## Experimental Section
Device Fabrication: Two kinds of tandem cells, series and parallel cells, were fabricated. Series tandem cells were fabricated by deposition of the PSBTBT- $\scriptstyle\cdot c o$ -PCDTBT:PC $_{70}\mathsf{B M}$ BHJ on the top ITO of the p-i-n a-Si cell, while parallel tandem cells were fabricated by deposition of the PSBTBT$_{c o\mathrm{-}\mathsf{P C D T B T:P C}_{70}\mathsf{B M\ B i}}$ HJ on the top ITO of the n-i-p a-Si cell. The various layers that make up the a-Si solar cell; namely $\mathsf{p}$ -doped hydrogenated a-Si, an intermediate layer of hydrogenated a-Si, n-doped hydrogenated a-Si, and a top ITO layer; were sequentially deposited and suitably etched to expose the bottom ITO layer. Single-junction polymer BHJ solar cells were then fabricated on an ITO-coated glass substrate by sequential solution state deposition of the following layers: a PEDOT:PSS (Baytron PH) conducting interlayer, a BHJ layer consisting of a low band-gap polymer PSBTBT-co-PCDTBT and [6,6]-phenyl $\mathsf C_{71}$ butyric acid methyl ester $(P C_{70}\mathsf{B M})$ , and a titanium suboxide optical spacer. After assembly of the tandem cell, an aluminum (Al) electrode was deposited by thermal evaporation. Before deposition of the BHJ layer, the a-Si cell was first cleaned with detergent, acetone, and isopropyl alcohol, and subsequently dried. PEDOT:PSS (Baytron PH) was spin-cast from aqueous solution to form a film of thickness of $40\mathsf{n m}$ . The substrate was dried for $10\min$ at $140^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in air and then transferred into a glove box to spin-cast the charge separation layer. A solution containing a mixture of PSBTBT- $.c o$ -PCDTBT:PC70BM (1:4) in dichlorobenzene solvent with concentration of $7\ \mathrm{wt}\%\ \mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ was then spin-cast on top of the PEDOT/ PSS layer. The $\mathsf{T i O}_{x}$ precursor solution was spin-cast in air on top of the
PCDTBT: $\mathsf{P C}_{70}\mathsf{B M}$ layer. The sample was heated at $140^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $10\min$ in air. Then, an aluminum (Al, $\mathsf{l o o}\mathsf{n m})$ electrode was deposited by thermal evaporation in a vacuum of about $5\times10^{-7}$ Torr. Current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ characteristics of the devices were measured using a Keithley 236 Source Measure Unit. The illumination was done using an Air Mass 1.5 Global $(\mathsf{A M}\ \mathsf{1.5G})$ solar simulator with an irradiation intensity of ${\mathsf{l}}000\ \not\ W\ {\mathsf{m}}^{-2}$ . The aperture $(12.7~\mathsf{m m}^{2})$ is used on top of the pixel to remove extrinsic effects, such as cross-talking, waveguide, shadow effect, and so on. Spectral mismatching factor is calculated by comparison of solar simulator spectrum with AM 1.5G spectrum at room temperature.
